Here's the technical choice I'd favor when we get to it: use Kokoro to create the voices, but initially let SGG play prerecorded .wav files.
Kokoro's model is released under the Apache 2.0 license, and its official model card includes an example that saves synthesized speech as WAV audio. We'd still check the licenses of any bundled dependencies before distributing the game.
Hugging Face
That gives us a nice, lightweight arrangement:
The game detects that the vendor has been blinded.
The event triggers his special dialogue.
Pygame plays the matching voice clip.
No speech model running continuously in the background, no unnecessary memory burden, and no delay while the vendor generates each sentence. We can give different NPCs different voices and personalities, too.
And about that Doctor Who clip… 😏 The homage is delightful, but I'd generate our own performance of the line rather than simply lift the BBC recording. The BBC says protected programme clips generally require permission, and it may not own every underlying right.
bbc.com.im
We can preserve the joke without making our little indie game dependent on permission for somebody else's audio.
Later, we could make voice generation an optional feature, or add more dialogue without changing the game's basic speech system.
