The first quest: The Mage's Love Potion
The objective
The court mage wants a love potion. The player needs to deliver one. The mage doesn't care how it was obtained—or at least, that's our starting rule.
Route A: The honest grind
Collect herbs and flowers, make ordinary potions, sell them, earn gold, buy the ingredients, and craft the love potion. This is the legitimate economy working as designed.
Route B: The bucket trick
The potion is already sitting on a shelf in BrightRun. The player discovers how to get it without paying the ridiculous price. Quest solved!
Route C: The Purple Passion Pineapple
The special ingredient, PPP, can be purchased from Riften's alchemist—or stolen from the shop shelf. The player can then use it to make the required potion.
Route D: The Uber Boss
The PPP can also be found in a cave guarded by a terrifying boss. Perhaps you can't beat the boss—but you can exploit movement, drink ten bowls of veggie soup, grab the pineapple, and RUN LIKE HELL. 🏃‍♂️💨
Route E: The economy exploit
Find a way to generate ridiculous amounts of gold and just buy the potion. No stealing, no boss fight, no flower-picking career required.
Here's the architectural idea hiding inside all this
These aren't five different quests. They're five different solution paths to the same quest.
The objective remains simple:
QUEST OBJECTIVE: Deliver Love Potion
The world provides different ways to achieve it. The player discovers which routes are available, which exploits they understand, and how much effort they want to expend.
And because exploits can change during a playthrough, the available routes can change too. Perhaps the bucket trick gets patched, but the veggie soup exploit is still available. Perhaps crafting becomes the lucrative loophole. The quest doesn't need to be rewritten every time a rule changes.
That's particularly useful for our first release. We can concentrate development on a small number of solid systems—movement, objects, inventory, vendors, prices, crafting, quests, and exploits—and let those systems combine into different solutions.
One quest. Many ways to break the world. That's a far more interesting first milestone than ten shallow quests that each permit only one solution.
And I can already see the player standing outside that boss's cave, looking at the pineapple, glancing at their ten bowls of soup, and thinking:
“I have made a terrible decision. But I have made it with STYLE.” 😂🥣🍍

