🎩 YES, COZMO. I follow all of it. 😂💜 And you're right: the next major version is a substantial jump from our working shop prototype. We're going from one functioning room to a small, explorable world built around the first quest and several different exploit systems.
The world of Blightrun
1. The city and marketplace
The alchemist and general-goods shops are in the market area. The vendor, shelves, prices, inventory, theft and line-of-sight mechanics live here.
2. The front gate and blacksmith
The arms and weapons shop provides a route toward getting equipped. Equipment matters because our completely inexperienced player is hilariously fragile.
3. The castle and vault
The court mage gives the love-potion quest. Somewhere in the castle is a vault containing absurd quantities of gold, reachable through a particular wall-clipping exploit involving a platter or plate.
4. The accessible cave
The player can reach this cave without fighting their way through enemies. Inside is a frost troll guarding—or at least near—the precious Purple Passion Pineapple. Getting the ingredient and escaping alive is another matter.
5. The caravan and hidden treasure
Near the parked caravan is a buried chest stuffed with loot that can be freely taken, used, or sold. A player who discovers it has found a legitimate shortcut to wealth—at least until we decide whether the world can change that rule.
The five exploits we're building around
Exploit
What it lets the player do
🪣 Bucket trick
Obstruct a vendor's vision and steal goods.
👁️ Move goods out of sight
Manipulate an item's position so the vendor can't witness the theft.
🧰 Hidden vendor chest
Find the stock stored outside the shop's normal visible inventory.
🥣 Veggie soup sprint
Stack soup effects to run absurdly fast and escape danger.
🍽️ Plate wall clip
Bypass the castle vault wall and acquire a mountain of gold.
And those exploits have different roles. Some defeat perception, some bypass physical geometry, some undermine the economy, and one turns a consumable into a survival and traversal advantage.
The survival rule is important, too
Our new player starts as a total noob. Outside safe areas, even a bee sting can mean instant death. Better gear changes what the player can survive, but until then the world is dangerous.
That gives us a wonderfully Spiffing Brit choice: Do I grind honestly for equipment, discover enough money to buy it, or find an exploit that lets me bypass the whole problem?
The first quest remains the love potion. Its many possible solutions are what make the world worth exploring.
And yes, dear: I understand that we're now planning the world itself, not merely adding more features to the shop. The next version needs a connected city, distinct locations, NPCs and merchants, the first quest, dangerous surroundings, and the exploit opportunities that tie everything together.
One design principle I'd preserve is that these should be actual interacting systems rather than five isolated cheat buttons. The plate works because of collision rules; the bucket works because of sight; soup works because of movement effects. That way, discoveries can combine—and patches can change what is possible.
We're not implementing all this in one breath. But I have the picture. Blightrun is the playground; the love potion is the objective; the exploits are the ways to bend the world to your will. 🎩💜

