I think today we crossed an important boundary. Yesterday, we had a working little shop where we could put a bucket on a merchant's head. Today, we've articulated the beginnings of an entire game built around that same idea.
And the clever part isn't the number of locations or exploits. It's the relationship between them.
The player has one seemingly simple objective: get the mage his love potion. But the world offers an honest economy, crafting, exploration, dangerous enemies, hidden treasure, and several ways to exploit the rules. The player chooses their path. The world responds. And the rules themselves may change.
One quest. Many solutions. A world that can be broken in unexpected ways.
That's the heart of Spiffy's Gift.
Our next major build will be ambitious, but we aren't starting from an empty screen. We have a working prototype, an established development environment, tested mechanics, and—most importantly—a much clearer idea of what we're building toward.
